WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.813
A domain name provides a human-readable label within the Domain Name System.

00:00:04.813 --> 00:00:08.347
A registered domain can contain subdomains such as app.

00:00:08.347 --> 00:00:08.997
example.

00:00:08.997 --> 00:00:10.175
com or mail.

00:00:10.175 --> 00:00:10.825
example.

00:00:10.825 --> 00:00:11.475
com.

00:00:11.475 --> 00:00:15.010
Organizations use naming to organize services, environments, regions, products,

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:16.188
and third-party integrations.

00:00:16.188 --> 00:00:17.759
Registrars manage registration relationships,

00:00:17.759 --> 00:00:21.686
while name servers publish authoritative DNS information for a domain.

00:00:21.686 --> 00:00:24.043
Administrative arrangements can change over time.

00:00:24.043 --> 00:00:26.792
Public registration details may be privacy-protected, incomplete,

00:00:26.792 --> 00:00:31.112
or maintained by a service provider rather than the operational team.

00:00:31.112 --> 00:00:35.039
Naming patterns can help defenders recognize likely services or environments,

00:00:35.039 --> 00:00:37.003
but labels are not proof.

00:00:37.003 --> 00:00:40.930
A name containing dev may host production traffic, remain unused,

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:42.894
or point to a vendor.

00:00:42.894 --> 00:00:46.821
Analysts should document the literal name separately from any interpretation.

00:00:46.821 --> 00:00:50.355
Abandoned or forgotten names create operational and brand risk.

00:00:50.355 --> 00:00:53.497
Records can outlive projects, vendors, or cloud resources.

00:00:53.497 --> 00:00:57.032
A stale name may confuse customers, expose old documentation,

00:00:57.032 --> 00:01:01.352
or point toward infrastructure no longer governed by the expected owner.

00:01:01.352 --> 00:01:02.923
Domain data is time-sensitive.

00:01:02.923 --> 00:01:06.457
Registrations, name servers, DNS answers, and ownership arrangements change.

00:01:06.457 --> 00:01:08.814
Preserve observation dates and historical context.

00:01:08.814 --> 00:01:15.097
A current lookup should not be used to rewrite what was true during an earlier event.

00:01:15.097 --> 00:01:20.595
Use domain information to form defensible questions: Is the asset in the internal inventory?

00:01:20.595 --> 00:01:22.559
Is there an assigned owner?

00:01:22.559 --> 00:01:24.523
Is the record still needed?

00:01:24.523 --> 00:01:28.843
Confirm through authorized organizational records before labeling a domain as rogue,

00:01:28.843 --> 00:01:30.820
compromised, or controlled by a specific party.
